I think it makes sense for this community to document ggplot and I would ask for some protocol for doing so as I can think of several things I'd immediately jump up and help document... actually, these are in other packages such as reshape as well, where I'm specifically thinking of the id.var argument which still remains obscure in documentation.

It would also be tremendous if examples hashed out in the group could easily be added to the examples in the documentation, or there could be "further examples" or perhaps just links to threads on here.

Ggplot has interesting documentation because what's in the pdf and in the help files (e.g. ?stat_boxplot) is a mere shadow of what's on the web page. Who uses the former?? I think it makes sense for the user community to have wiki version of the web documentation. Another possibility is having a repo on git hub for the html or having it in markdown. It would be a trememdous thing to be able to convert this into .Rd files. However, I'm not sure how that would interface with Roxygen documentation or if one would win out. I know almost nothing about Roxygen, other than these are ideas which might make it into Hadley's branch.

my 2 cents.

James

> I suppose the onus is on us to figure these things out and throw them up on the wiki or something. Perhaps the source will be easier to read in the future with the rewrite or the documentation will be better, but if you watch that google video I linked a few days ago Hadley is clearly torn between supporting this "old" package and researching novel methods of plotting information.
